WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.120
Here are four quotes and one statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.640
After hearing them, let us know if&nbsp;
you agree or disagree and why. Ready?

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.800
“Abortion is legal because babies can’t vote.”
“No woman can call herself free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.720
who does not control her own body.”
“Race and skin color are socially constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.440
not biologically natural.”
“If liberty means anything at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.800
it means the right to tell people&nbsp;
what they do not want to hear.”&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.080
And finally: “We are educators&nbsp;
and the world ain’t round!”

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.720
To optimize for learning and retention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.080
pause the video, write how you agree or disagree&nbsp;
in the comments below, and then continue.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:59.040
When you discuss a topic and everyone agrees, the&nbsp;
conversation often dies out quickly. But when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.960
disagree, you're putting yourself in opposition&nbsp;
to what was said. You take risks and have skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.880
in the game. This can bring the discussion to a&nbsp;
level where everyone involved learns more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.200
the issue. If we are going to disagree, we should&nbsp;
be careful to do it well. But what does that mean?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.680
In 2008 Paul Graham attempted&nbsp;
to answer this question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.840
by proposing a hierarchy with 7 stages of&nbsp;
disagreement ranging from weak to strong.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:40.000
The lowest level is name-calling which attacks the&nbsp;
person and not what they say. If Bob and Jen say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.920
“We are educators and the world ain’t&nbsp;
round”. You may answer “you are idiots!”.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.680
Second is the ad hominem attack, which&nbsp;
questions the authority of someone without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:00.160
addressing the argument. If they say, “We&nbsp;
are educators and the world ain’t round”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:06.240
you can respond “you may be educators,&nbsp;
but you certainly aren’t scientists”. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.840
name calling it’s a weak form of disagreement&nbsp;
as it attacks them but not what they say.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.600
Third is responding to tone, meaning&nbsp;
to criticize the style of language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:25.920
rather than its content. If they say, “We&nbsp;
are educators and the world ain’t round”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.800
you can reply: “Ain’t is bad English. First&nbsp;
learn to speak properly!”. It is a weak form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.120
of disagreement, as you object to the tone and&nbsp;
not the content. You’re still not saying much.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.640
Fourth is contradiction. Now we&nbsp;
get responses to what was said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:53.760
rather than by whom or how. If they say, “We&nbsp;
are educators and the world ain’t round”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.000
you can retort: “It is round!”. Contradiction&nbsp;
can have weight, because sometimes seeing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.640
opposing case stated explicitly is enough to get&nbsp;
the point across. But this is not always the case.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.400
The counterargument is a contradiction with&nbsp;
reasoning and the first form of convincing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:21.200
disagreement. If they say, “We are educators&nbsp;
and the world ain’t round”, you can answer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:27.040
“I’m a scientist and it isn’t flat either!&nbsp;
In fact, from space it looks pretty round”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.680
When aimed squarely at the original argument,&nbsp;
it can be convincing. But often two people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.040
are arguing passionately about something are&nbsp;
actually talking about two different things.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.160
The refutation is quoting someone&nbsp;
and explaining why they are mistaken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.400
If they say, “We are educators and the&nbsp;
world ain’t round” you may respond:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.520
“When you say that you are an educator and&nbsp;
the world ain’t round, you actually imply that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.000
you have authority and that the world is flat.&nbsp;
Videos from space are proof it's pretty round.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.480
It’s a convincing form of disagreement. It's&nbsp;
also a rare form, because it's more work.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.760
Refuting the central point is the most powerful&nbsp;
form of disagreement. Prior to this level you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.080
have been unclear or, in the worst case,&nbsp;
deliberately dishonest. If they say, “We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.880
are educators and the world ain’t round” you can&nbsp;
refute the central point as such: “Your statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.200
that the earth is not round is technically right&nbsp;
— the earth being closer to an ellipsoid — “

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.200
“But, by only sharing what the earth is not&nbsp;
without adding any clarification you lead people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.040
to believe whatever fits best with their own&nbsp;
convictions, provided it is not that the earth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:57.760
round. Assuming educators are meant to help people&nbsp;
understand better, this is in direct contradiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.280
with the idea of you being an educator and&nbsp;
thus makes your statement an oxymoron.”

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.800
Graham stated that there are 3 main&nbsp;
benefits of knowing forms of disagreement.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:19.760
It helps us to evaluate what we read, see&nbsp;
and hear. In particular, it helps us spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.640
intellectually dishonest arguments&nbsp;
from speakers who use words eloquently.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.000
It makes conversations richer, because&nbsp;
participants focus on the actual point.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.240
It makes us happier. Lower levels of&nbsp;
disagreement attack the person, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:47.040
is mean. Higher levels of disagreement attack the&nbsp;
idea, which is easier to accept and assimilate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.520
Most people don't really enjoy being obnoxious;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.680
they sometimes are because they haven't found a&nbsp;
better way to express themselves. If moving up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:06.320
the disagreement hierarchy makes people less mean,&nbsp;
it will probably make many of them happier too.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.240
What are your thoughts? Do you&nbsp;
agree or disagree with Graham?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.680
If you don’t feel strongly about this&nbsp;
topic, you’ll probably disagree with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:21.600
one of the other four statements we made at the&nbsp;
beginning. But can you refute the central point?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.600
We will now repeat them. Let us know how YOU&nbsp;
disagree with them in the comments below.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.720
“Abortion is legal because babies can’t vote.”
“No woman can call herself free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:43.120
who does not control her own body.”
“Race and skin colour are socially constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.480
not biologically natural.”
“If liberty means anything at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:07:01.840
it means the right to tell people&nbsp;
what they do not want to hear.”

